✅ What is WAF?

WAF = Web Application Firewall

A security layer that sits in front of your web app / API and filters
HTTP/HTTPS traffic to block common web attacks — before they hit your Spring Boot (or other) servers.

Traditional firewall  → mostly IP / port / protocol (network layer)
WAF                   → understands HTTP requests (URL, headers, body, cookies)


Where it sits

  User / Internet
        ↓
     CDN (optional)
        ↓
       WAF              ← inspects HTTP
        ↓
  Load Balancer / Ingress
        ↓
  Application (Pods / VMs)
        ↓
       DB


🎯 What does a WAF protect against? (OWASP-style)

Common examples:
  - SQL Injection (SQLi)
  - Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)
  - Remote file inclusion / path traversal
  - Bad bots / scrapers (partly)
  - Some Layer-7 abuse (known bad patterns, rate rules)
  - CSRF / protocol violations (depending on rules)
  - Known attack signatures / CVE patterns

It does NOT replace:
  - Secure coding
  - AuthN/AuthZ in the app
  - Network DDoS scrubbing for huge volumetric floods (need CDN/DDoS service)


🛡️ How it works (simple)

1) Request arrives
2) WAF checks against rules / signatures / anomaly models
3) Decision:
     ALLOW  → forward to app
     BLOCK  → 403 / challenge
     LOG    → monitor (detection mode)
     RATE-LIMIT / CAPTCHA → slow down bots

Modes:
  Detection / monitoring → log only (safe to start)
  Prevention / blocking  → actively deny bad traffic


🧰 Rule types (interview)

  Signature-based  → match known bad patterns (e.g. ' OR 1=1 --)
  Allow / deny lists → IP, geo, user-agent
  Rate limiting    → too many requests from one IP
  Bot management   → JS challenge, CAPTCHA, bot score
  Custom rules     → protect /login, /otp, admin paths


🔄 WAF vs Firewall vs DDoS protection

Traditional Firewall
  → ports, IPs, network protocols (L3/L4 mostly)

WAF
  → HTTP application attacks (L7)

DDoS protection / scrubbing
  → absorb massive floods (bandwidth / connection exhaustion)
  → WAF helps some L7 floods; big volumetric attacks need edge/CDN


Cloud examples (names only)
  AWS WAF, Azure WAF, Cloudflare WAF, Akamai, Imperva
  Often combined with CDN + DDoS


🔹 For Java / backend interviews (practical)

- Put public APIs behind CDN + WAF when exposed to internet
- Extra strict rules on sensitive routes: /login, /otp, /admin
- App still needs:
    parameterized queries (against SQLi)
    output encoding (against XSS)
    auth, validation, rate limits
- WAF is defense-in-depth — not the only control
- False positives: tune rules so real users aren’t blocked
- Logs from WAF help incident investigation


⚠️ Important points
- WAF ≠ antivirus for servers
- Encrypted HTTPS: WAF must terminate TLS (or see decrypted traffic) to inspect bodies
- Internal-only services may not need a public WAF
- Misconfigured WAF can block legit traffic or miss attacks


✍️ Sample Interview Answer
"A WAF is a Web Application Firewall that filters HTTP/HTTPS traffic in front of the app.
It blocks common attacks like SQL injection and XSS using rules and signatures.
We usually place it at the edge with a CDN. It complements — not replaces — secure coding,
and for large volumetric DDoS we still rely on CDN/scrubbing, while the WAF handles application-layer threats."


🧠 Interview one-liner
“A WAF is an HTTP-aware firewall in front of your web app that blocks OWASP-style attacks like SQLi and XSS before they reach the application.”
